   $q$Divide $x^3 + 2x^2 - 5$ by $x^2 + 1$. Which shows the result in the form $q(x) + \dfrac{r(x)}{x^2 + 1}$, where $r(x)$ has degree less than 2?$q$, $q$[{"text":"$x + 2 - \\dfrac{7}{x^2 + 1}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"omitted-zero-placeholder"},{"text":"$x + 2 - \\dfrac{x + 7}{x^2 + 1}$","is_correct":true},{"text":"$x + 2 + \\dfrac{x + 7}{x^2 + 1}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"sign-error-on-remainder"},{"text":"$x + 2 + \\dfrac{x - 7}{x^2 + 1}$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"sign-error-subtracting-product"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$$x + 2 - \dfrac{x + 7}{x^2 + 1}$$q$, $q$Write the dividend as $x^3 + 2x^2 + 0x - 5$. First $x^3 \div x^2 = x$; subtract $x(x^2 + 1) = x^3 + x$ to leave $2x^2 - x - 5$. Next $2x^2 \div x^2 = 2$; subtract $2(x^2 + 1) = 2x^2 + 2$ to leave $-x - 7$. So the result is $x + 2 + \dfrac{-x - 7}{x^2 + 1} = x + 2 - \dfrac{x + 7}{x^2 + 1}$. The choice $x + 2 + \dfrac{x - 7}{x^2 + 1}$ adds $x$ instead of subtracting it in the first step. Since $x^2 + 1$ is never $0$, there is no domain restriction.$q$, 'draft', null)
